think The refusal is the loop working, never a fault. chatgpt-mind.rish asserts file-absent on .mind-state/CUSTODY at arm time and between laps, so a lap that meets a custody question leaves a sentinel naming it and the loop stays down until a hand reads it. Mind's sentinel, left 15:20: candidate-unbooked -- the card seats the Brushstroke-Surf lane but does not book the pure-Swift SkateCore crux before the AppKit-form, Skate-social, and DJINN-percent-six gates. Mind refused to invent its own crux booking, which is exactly the deference the charter asks of an unattended lap.
obs The gate was answered after the sentinel was written, in the role-swap round: construction/ITINERARY.md line 76 names SkateCore as MIND's crux in the Still-open block, and the six-bodies charter's Mind row says it plainly -- the pure-Swift SkateCore bounded core is the booked crux, with DJINN's design lead held at gate %6. The Choir round then proved the runway: skate_native_core GREEN on this macOS host, 21 Swift tests, 0 failures.
obs So the hand's act was a read and a rm: the sentinel came off, and the loop's own check mode ran GREEN end to end -- prompt, repository, tools, isolated login, host signing, power reading, outer jail. Mind arms with its own command from here; arming it is the operator's gesture, never this hand's.
obs Worth keeping: the sentinel shape works. A loop that leaves a named, specific question on disk and refuses to continue is a loop whose custody discipline survives its operator being asleep -- and the question it left was answerable from the living card alone.
